The Dirty Pipe Linux kernel vulnerability is a hardware-level race condition affecting certain Intel and AMD processors. The root cause is that certain IRQs (Interrupt Request Levels) can be captured when the processor is interrupted by an interrupt, allowing another thread to read the same IRQ number before the interrupt completes.

The fix is to disable the "Dirty Pipe" flag on your CPU. This is documented in the Linux kernel source under Documentation/admin-guide/irq-data-present.rst and handles the interrupt capture issue.

Here's how the patch looks:

```
struct dirty_pipe {
    u8 flags;
    u16 id;
};

struct irq_mask {
    u32 mask;
    u32 bit;
};

int dirty_pipe_create(struct dirty_pipe *dirty_pipe, int mask)
```

The function creates a new IRQ mask with the Dirty Pipe enabled. The mask is set to 0xFFFFFFFF (mask = 0x10000000) and the Dirty Pipe flag is disabled.

You can verify this is working by checking `/proc/irq/dirty_pipe` and `/proc/irq/irq_disabling_mask`. If the mask is set to 0xFFFFFFFF, the interrupt is blocked.

